Pancreatic Cancer
Haematology & Oncology
Painless obstructive jaundice, Unintentional weight loss, Palpable non-tender gallbladder, Pancreatic mass on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-037
Reason for Visit: Rapid development of profound jaundice, intractable pruritus, and severe, unrelenting epigastric pain radiating to the back.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an older adult experiencing a sudden deepening of jaundice, dark urine, and agonizing, deep abdominal pain that prevents sleep. Urgent in-person evaluation was clinically mandated to evaluate for acute biliary sepsis (cholangitis) versus rapidly progressive malignant obstruction, as delaying assessment until the next day posed a high risk of fatal biliary sepsis or uncontrolled pain.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is completely yellow, I am itching so badly I'm bleeding, and I have this deep, boring pain in my stomach that shoots straight through to my spine."
History of Present Illness: The 68-year-old patient reports an insidious 2-month history of a 10 kg unintentional significant weight loss and profound anorexia. They were recently diagnosed with new-onset atypical diabetes mellitus despite lacking classic risk factors. Over the past week, they noticed painless, progressive jaundice. Today, the jaundice deepened profoundly, accompanied by intractable pruritus, dark 'coca-cola' coloured urine, and pale, acholic (clay-coloured) stools. Tonight, they developed a deep, dull, poorly localized epigastric pain that radiates directly through to the mid-back and is completely unrelieved by changes in position.
Functional Status: Pacing the room in severe discomfort; unable to lie in bed due to the radiating back pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation and intense psychological distress due to the pruritus and pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Cachectic adult exhibiting profound generalized jaundice and prominent scleral icterus. Actively scratching their arms.
Physical Exam:
Abdomen: Scaphoid appearance. Deep palpation of the epigastrium elicits moderate, deep-seated tenderness without rigidity or rebound. A palpable, smooth, non-tender, and distinctly distended mass is located in the right upper quadrant (Courvoisier's sign - enlarged gallbladder). Hepatomegaly is noted.
Skin: Intense jaundice. Widespread linear excoriations on the limbs and trunk from severe scratching. A tender, erythematous, cord-like vein is palpable on the left forearm, consistent with migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (Trousseau's syndrome).
Lymphatic: A hard, fixed left supraclavicular lymph node (Virchow's node) is palpable.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty tubes of over-the-counter anti-itch cream on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent paracetamol overdose. No heavy alcohol use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pancreatic Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Biliary Obstruction and Pain Crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Cholangitis (Gallstone) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the palpable, completely non-tender gallbladder (Courvoisier's law), the massive weight loss, the presence of Virchow's node, and the lack of high spiking fevers/rigors (Charcot's triad).

Acute Pancreatitis (Alcohol/Gallstone) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the insidious, chronic progression of weight loss and painless jaundice prior to the acute pain episode, rather than a sudden, isolated acute inflammatory event.

Autoimmune Hepatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the classic mechanical obstructive picture (acholic stools, dark urine, dilated gallbladder) and the prominent radiating back pain suggesting retroperitoneal tumour invasion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an absolute textbook presentation of advanced carcinoma of the pancreatic head: painless obstructive jaundice, profound cachexia, new-onset diabetes, a palpable non-tender gallbladder (Courvoisier's sign), and Virchow's node. The acute escalation of severe, radiating back pain indicates tumour infiltration into the retroperitoneal splanchnic nerve plexus. The intense pruritus and dark urine confirm complete mechanical biliary obstruction. Delaying assessment would result in intolerable, unmitigated agony and an unacceptably high risk of ascending biliary sepsis. The urgent home visit was essential to rule out acute cholangitis, deliver potent analgesia, and arrange immediate hospital transfer for biliary decompression.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered subcutaneous morphine 5mg for the severe retroperitoneal pain. Immediate referral and ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent multiphase contrast-enhanced abdominal CT (anticipating a hypodense pancreatic mass and a 'double duct sign') and urgent gastroenterology review for ERCP and biliary stenting.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Apply cool, wet towels directly to the skin instead of scratching. By using cold temperature to numb the superficial nerve endings, you interrupt the severe itch-scratch cycle and prevent introducing a bacterial infection into your broken skin.

Lean forward while sitting if the back pain spikes. By flexing the spine forward, you slightly relieve the physical pressure the inflamed pancreas is placing on the nerves in your retroperitoneum.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is required. If the patient suddenly develops a high fever with uncontrollable shivering (rigors), or becomes confused, inform the paramedics immediately as this indicates a life-threatening infection of the blocked bile ducts.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe jaundice, enlarged gallbladder, and weight loss strongly point to a blockage in the pancreas; an urgent CT scan at the hospital is strictly required to confirm the exact nature of the mass.
Clinical Course: The blockage prevents bile from leaving the liver, forcing it into the skin and causing the intense itching. The hospital will likely place a small plastic tube (stent) into the duct to drain the bile and relieve the jaundice.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Wait for the ambulance. Do not apply heat packs to the abdomen. Try to sit leaning slightly forward.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A growth at the head of the pancreas has completely clamped shut the main bile pipe. This causes the severe yellowing, the pale stools, and the deep radiating nerve pain. The injection will blunt the nerve pain temporarily. The hospital requires urgent imaging to map the blockage and plan a procedure to unblock the duct.
Actionable Steps: Avoid scratching. Lean forward for comfort. Hand over all diabetes medications to the ambulance crew.

